INT. LAKE HOUSE, UPSTAIRS LANDING - NIGHT

A dark pine-paneled hallway. Moonlight through a window at the end. A grandfather clock ticks. Every door but one is closed.

A TALL FIGURE (silhouette, long coat, face hidden by shadow) stands motionless at the end of the hall.

WREN ACHTERBERG-NNAMDI (30s, bathrobe, flashlight) stands at the top of the stairs. She doesn't move. The figure doesn't move. The clock ticks.

WREN
(whispering)
Hello?

The figure tilts its head.

WREN (CONT'D)
Is that you, Dad?

No answer. The flashlight shakes in her hand. She raises it. The beam climbs the long coat, stops at the throat.

WREN (CONT'D)
(barely)
You're not supposed to be here.

The figure lifts an arm and points at the closed door beside her.

Wren's eyes slide to it. Back. The figure holds the gesture.

She reaches for the doorknob. Her hand trembles. She turns it.

INT. CHILD'S BEDROOM - CONTINUOUS

A narrow room with a toy chest and a narrow bed. A small BOY (6, pajamas, eyes wide) sits upright in the blankets, clutching a stuffed rabbit.

BOY
Mommy, he was in my closet.

Wren crouches. The boy buries his face in her robe. Behind them, in the doorway, the figure appears. Its shadow falls across both.

The figure steps forward and for the first time the moonlight catches its face. It's an old man. Her father. Wet-eyed, bewildered, in the wrong house.

OLD MAN
Wren?

WREN
Dad.

OLD MAN
I couldn't find the stairs.

She stands, still holding the boy. Her flashlight clatters to the floor, rolling in a slow circle.

WREN
(softly)
It's all right.

OLD MAN
I thought it was the other house.

WREN
I know.

She extends her free hand. He takes it. The boy peeks out from her robe and, with great solemnity, holds out the stuffed rabbit to his grandfather.

The clock chimes midnight.
